# Caddy v2 in front of the compose stack: automatic Let's Encrypt TLS, no certbot.
# Copy to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile and replace example.com with the host of SITE_URL (without www).
# DNS for example.com and www.example.com must point to this machine and ports 80 and 443 must be open.
# Next listens on 127.0.0.1:3000 (SITE_BIND and SITE_PORT in .env; change the reverse_proxy lines if you changed SITE_PORT).
#
# Body limits (the same as deploy/nginx.conf.example): 1 MB for everything, 6 MB for /admin/. Public pages send nothing
# over 16 KB, while the admin's image-upload Server Action POSTs to the page URL /admin/... with up to 5 MiB of image plus
# the multipart overhead. Next reads every multipart POST to every page up to 5 MiB before it knows whether it is a valid
# action, so the proxy is the place that stops junk POSTs.
#
# Notes:
# - Caddy keeps the Host header and sets X-Forwarded-For (to the client address, ignoring any value the client sent) and
#   X-Forwarded-Proto by itself, which is what TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1 expects. With Cloudflare in front of Caddy set
#   TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=2 and configure Caddy's trusted_proxies for Cloudflare's ranges: Caddy then keeps the X-Forwarded-For of a
#   request from those ranges and replaces it for any other caller, so a caller who reaches the origin directly is not believed.
#   Still let the origin accept Cloudflare alone (firewall allow-list or Authenticated Origin Pulls): it also keeps the WAF in the way.
# - Next sends the security headers itself, including Strict-Transport-Security (HSTS_MAX_AGE). Do not add them here.
# - Readers must use exactly the host of SITE_URL: the www block redirects the secondary host.
# - Caddy has no request rate limit and no response cache of its own: both are plugins (for example the caddy-ratelimit module,
#   built with xcaddy). The limits and the one-hour picture cache that deploy/nginx.conf.example sets are the ones to copy if you add them,
#   or put a CDN with a rate rule in front (docs/INSTALL.md, "A CDN in front"). Next limits the same paths per address too
#   (docs/SECURITY.md, "Rate limits"), and keeps its page cache in memory, bounded by its in-memory LRU (cacheMaxMemorySize).

# Logs. Caddy writes no access log until a `log` directive is added to a site, but its error log, which is on by default,
# names the request, query string and Referer included, whenever the upstream fails (a search term would sit in the journal
# with the reader's address). The global `log` below filters that one: the query is cut from the uri and the Referer (the
# search URL after a click on a result) is dropped. If you add `log` to the site block, give it the same `format filter`
# block: a site log does not inherit this one. Keep the journal short-lived (journald: MaxRetentionSec=7day).
{
	log {
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>uri regexp \?.* ?
				request>headers>Referer delete
			}
		}
	}
}

www.example.com {
	redir https://example.com{uri} permanent
}

example.com {
	# Keeps the one-time OAuth code (callback query string), the newsletter tokens and anything typed into the admin
	# login form out of the access log: these paths are not logged at all, even if a `log` directive is added later.
	@private path /api/auth/github/callback /newsletter/* /*/newsletter/* /api/newsletter/* /admin/login /admin/login/*
	log_skip @private

	# TRACE, TRACK and CONNECT make Next throw a 500 and write a stack trace to its log for every request (audit issue 103). Nothing on
	# this site uses them, so they stop here, before the other handlers.
	@badmethod method TRACE TRACK CONNECT
	handle @badmethod {
		respond 405
	}

	handle /admin/* {
		request_body {
			max_size 6MB
		}
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
	}

	handle {
		request_body {
			max_size 1MB
		}
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
	}
}
